================================================================================
Algorithms and technical acknowledgements
================================================================================

The following entries acknowledge short algorithms and mathematical techniques
implemented in the project source. They are not separately bundled libraries,
and these acknowledgements do not alter the project's GNU GPL v3 licensing.

Björn Ottosson - Oklab

The linear-sRGB/Oklab conversion matrices are from "A perceptual color space
for image processing". The accompanying conversion code is offered by its
author in the public domain or, alternatively, under the MIT License.
This project relies on the author's public-domain dedication; the upstream
MIT alternative remains available. The matrices appear in palette_sampling.glsl,
vk_2_map_iter_stripe.comp, vk_slope.frag, ShdPaletteAttribute.h and
TimelineEvaluator.cpp.
https://bottosson.github.io/posts/oklab/

Krzysztof Narkowicz - ACES curve fit

The ACES (Narkowicz fit) tone-map option uses Narkowicz's compact curve fit
to the ACES RRT+ODT, not the ACES transform itself. The published curve code is
offered under CC0 or the MIT License.
This project relies on the CC0 option for this curve fit; the upstream MIT
alternative remains available. This does not license the full ACES transforms.
https://knarkowicz.wordpress.com/2016/01/06/aces-filmic-tone-mapping-curve/

Jorge Jimenez - Interleaved Gradient Noise

The dithering noise constants follow "Next Generation Post Processing in Call
of Duty: Advanced Warfare", presented at SIGGRAPH 2014. What is taken is the
three published constants and the fract() form around them, in
shdsrc/vk_linear_interpolation.frag, shdsrc/vk_2_map_iter_stripe.comp and
shdsrc/vk_fog.frag. The
presentation attaches no license statement to the technique. The current
implementation's separate Alan Wolfe MIT grant is identified in the
"Interleaved Gradient Noise - licensed replacement expression" entry below.
https://www.advances.realtimerendering.com/s2014/index.html

John Hable - Uncharted 2 filmic curve

The Filmic tone-map option follows the curve presented in "Uncharted 2: HDR
Lighting" at GDC 2010 and restated on the author's blog. What is taken is the
rational form of the curve and its six published constants, in
shdsrc/vk_linear_interpolation.frag. Neither the talk nor the blog post attaches
a license statement to the curve. The current implementation's separate
Sascha Willems MIT grant is identified in the "Hable curve - licensed
replacement implementation" entry below.
https://gdcvault.com/play/1012459/Uncharted-2-HDR
https://filmicworlds.com/blog/filmic-tonemapping-operators/

Erik Reinhard, Michael Stark, Peter Shirley, and James Ferwerda

The extended Reinhard tone-map option follows "Photographic Tone Reproduction
for Digital Images", SIGGRAPH 2002.
https://doi.org/10.1145/566654.566575

Peter Markstein - FMA division correction

The correctly rounded double division built from a hoisted reciprocal, in
shdsrc/vk_2_map_iter_stripe.comp and shdsrc/palette_sampling.glsl (included by
shdsrc/vk_iteration_palette.frag), is the
two-FMA residual-and-correction step Markstein published in "Computation of
elementary functions on the IBM RISC System/6000 processor", IBM Journal of
Research and Development 34(1), 1990, and restated with Alan H. Karp in
"High-Precision Division and Square Root", ACM TOMS 23(4), 1997. The papers
attach no license statement to the technique.
https://doi.org/10.1147/rd.341.0111
https://doi.org/10.1145/279232.279237

Chris Wellons - lowbias32

The pattern-noise integer hash uses lowbias32 from hash-prospector, distributed
under the Unlicense.
https://github.com/skeeto/hash-prospector
The permission text is retained in extern/licenses/lowbias32-UNLICENSE.txt
and third-party/licenses/bundled/lowbias32-UNLICENSE.txt; release staging copies
the extern/licenses text into the binary bundle. Source and permission checked
on 2026-09-30 at the repository's master URLs; this is not a pinned commit.

Iñigo Quilez - cosine palettes

Procedural palette presets use the cosine-palette technique described in
"Palettes".
https://iquilezles.org/articles/palettes/

The RandomSmooth generator in src/rff2/preset/shader/palette/ShdPalettePresets.cpp
uses the short a + b*cos(2*pi*(c*t+d)) form with randomized frequencies and
phases. Quilez's linked Palettes shader ll2GD3 carries an archived original-author
MIT grant for its cosine function and seven examples; see "Archive permission
findings and owner clarification - 2026-10-04" and
extern/licenses/iquilez-palettes-MIT.txt. This does not license arbitrary
article text or unrelated code. The separate Erkaman MIT counterpart is
also retained below; neither reference establishes the actual historical donor.

RandomSmooth [10-20] reuses the existing RandomSmooth color generator and only
sets a shorter iteration interval; it introduces no copied third-party code.

Edwin Catmull and Raphael Rom - uniform cubic interpolation

src/rff2/video/TimelineSchedule.cpp (catmullRom) and
src/rff2/video/TimelineEvaluator.cpp (cubic) evaluate the standard uniform
Catmull-Rom polynomial, with endpoint tangents (p2-p0)/2 and (p3-p1)/2.
Mathematical reference: "A Class of Local Interpolating Splines", Computer
Aided Geometric Design, 1974, pp. 317-326. Historical reference and derivation:
https://alvyray.com/Memos/CG/Pixar/spline77.pdf
https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/direct3d9/d3dxvec3catmullrom
These references identify the mathematics; they do not assert that Microsoft
or Lucasfilm source code is incorporated or supply a separate code license.

Quintic smootherstep - mathematical reference to Ken Perlin

The eased_cycle functions in shdsrc/palette_sampling.glsl and
shdsrc/vk_2_map_iter_stripe.comp, samplePaletteStops in
src/rff2/attr/ShdPaletteAttribute.h and iterationColoringCurve in
src/rff2/ui/workspace/ChoiceEditors.hpp (which draws the Iteration Coloring
tiles) use 6*t^5 - 15*t^4 + 10*t^3. Ken Perlin's
"Improving Noise" (2002), section 3, describes this interpolation polynomial:
https://mrl.cs.nyu.edu/~perlin/paper445.pdf
It also follows from endpoint values 0 and 1 with zero first and second
derivatives at both ends. This concerns the polynomial, not a Perlin noise
library, permutation table, or license to copy the paper's text.

Additional mathematical references - project implementation licenses unchanged

These references identify mathematics or related techniques; they do not assert
that the cited authors' source code was copied, grant rights to their articles,
or change the licenses of independently written project implementations.

- ExpressionParser.cpp uses an operator-stack/postfix parsing approach related
  to Edsger W. Dijkstra's "ALGOL 60 Translation" (1961), shunting-yard parsing:
  https://ir.cwi.nl/pub/9251
- Perturbator.h implements log-log escape smoothing, with bailout normalization
  and a locally estimated degree in the general-formula variant. Background:
  Linas Vepstas, "Renormalizing the Mandelbrot Escape" (1997):
  https://linas.org/art-gallery/escape/escape.html
- The Burning Ship examples/RIABS operation in ExpressionParser.cpp/.h and
  CallbackFractal.cpp refer to Michael Michelitsch and Otto E. Roessler,
  "The burning ship and its quasi-Julia sets" (1992):
  https://doi.org/10.1016/0097-8493(92)90032-Q
  CallbackFractal.cpp also explains the smoothing used by Perturbator.h.
- effects.glsl uses a normal/half-vector power highlight. vk_slope.frag uses
  an artistic variation. Background: Jim Blinn, "Models of Light Reflection
  for Computer Synthesized Pictures" (1977); these are not claims of a complete
  physically based model:
  https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/publication/models-of-light-reflection-for-computer-synthesized-pictures/
- RFFMapCompression.cpp uses a two-dimensional Lorenzo predictor on bit
  patterns with reversible residual coding. Reference: Lawrence Ibarria,
  Peter Lindstrom, Jarek Rossignac and Andrzej Szymczak, "Out-of-core compression
  and decompression of large n-dimensional scalar fields" (2003):
  https://doi.org/10.1111/1467-8659.00681
  The paper builds on earlier parallelogram prediction; the full compression
  pipeline here is not identified as a copied implementation of that paper.
- TimelineSchedule.cpp uses adaptive Simpson integration, a textbook numerical
  quadrature method. Reference for Simpson's rule and error term: NIST DLMF,
  section 3.5, equations 3.5.6 and 3.5.8:
  https://dlmf.nist.gov/3.5
- RenderScene.cpp uses fixed-tile boundary-based filling, related to the
  Mariani-Silver approach. It does not implement recursive subdivision and
  should not be described as the full Mariani-Silver algorithm. Background:
  https://ricomariani.medium.com/the-mariani-silver-algorithm-for-drawing-the-mandelbrot-set-a71e31bc20b6
- TimelineWindow.cpp selects decimal 1/2/5 tick intervals. Background: Paul S.
  Heckbert, "Nice Numbers for Graph Labels", Graphics Gems (1990), pp. 61-63:
  https://www.realtimerendering.com/resources/GraphicsGems/gems.html
  Similarity to the published short rule does not establish a code-copying
  history. No Graphics Gems implementation license is assigned to local code.
- The bundled stb_image decoder implements PNG Paeth filtering and Adam7
  interlacing, specified by PNG. These format references supplement, not
  replace, stb_image's retained public-domain and IJG notices:
  https://www.w3.org/TR/2003/REC-PNG-20031110/

Gauss-Legendre quadrature - mathematical reference

src/rff2/video/TimelineAnimationPhases.hpp uses two- and four-point rules to
integrate animation rates. The two-point nodes are +/-1/sqrt(3), with unit
weights. The four-point node magnitudes are sqrt((3 +/- 2*sqrt(6/5))/7);
outer and inner weights are (18-sqrt(30))/36 and (18+sqrt(30))/36 respectively.
These mathematical values were checked against the constants on 2026-09-30.
Verification reference: NIST DLMF, section 3.5(v), Gauss-Legendre formula:
https://dlmf.nist.gov/3.5#v
This is a mathematical reference, not a claim that NIST code was copied.

