HDR and display transfer functions - IEC, ITU-R, SMPTE, and ARIB

The HDR video export implements published colorimetry and transfer functions
from openly documented standards. Only the parameters needed to implement the
functions are used; no standards text is reproduced, and none of these bodies
endorses, sponsors, or certifies this program.

  ITU-R BT.709 and BT.2020    Primaries behind the linear-light matrix in
                              shdsrc/vk_linear_interpolation.frag, and the
                              bt2020 / bt2020nc tags on the encoder.
  IEC 61966-2-1 (sRGB)        EOTF and its inverse, used to move between stored
                              code values and linear light in
                              shdsrc/vk_linear_interpolation.frag,
                              shdsrc/vk_bloom.frag,
                              shdsrc/vk_static_2_image.frag, and
                              src/rff2/video/TimelineEvaluator.cpp.
                              Linear RGB palette interpolation also uses this
                              transfer in src/rff2/attr/ShdPaletteAttribute.h,
                              shdsrc/vk_iteration_palette.frag and
                              shdsrc/vk_2_map_iter_stripe.comp.
                              shdsrc/vk_smooth_present.frag decodes display
                              values for an sRGB swapchain attachment.
  SMPTE ST 2084 (PQ)          Inverse EOTF in vk_linear_interpolation.frag and
                              src/rff2/ui/VideoWindow.cpp. Also published as
                              ITU-R BT.2100.
  SMPTE ST 2086               Form of the master-display metadata the PQ export
                              passes to the encoder.
  ARIB STD-B67 (HLG)          OETF in the same two files, developed by the BBC
                              and NHK and also published as ITU-R BT.2100.

The "HDR10 (PQ)" option is named for the profile it targets - 10-bit BT.2020
with the ST 2084 transfer and static ST 2086 metadata. HDR10 is an open profile
with no certification mark; nothing here claims conformance testing.

Additional implementation locations: src/rff2/video/ZoomOverlay.cpp uses
sRGB/PQ/HLG encode/decode and the BT.709-to-BT.2020 matrix;
shdsrc/vk_color.frag and shdsrc/vk_stripe.frag use the sRGB transfer;
shdsrc/palette_sampling.glsl supplies shared palette transfer helpers;
shdsrc/vk_slope.frag uses sRGB conversion for Oklab shading/tint;
shdsrc/mfr_display.glsl uses BT.2020 luminance and the inverse gamut matrix.
PQ/HLG parameter reference: https://www.itu.int/rec/R-REC-BT.2100

================================================================================
Project procedural effects, Chaos blur, and supplied MFR display equations
================================================================================

shdsrc/effects.glsl and shdsrc/dark_finish.glsl identify their integer mixer
as original project code. It combines cell/seed values with 197, 1999, 101
and 17, then uses shifts 11 and 7 and multipliers 7919 and 104729. Lattice
values are blended with cubic smoothstep. This is separate from the credited
lowbias32 routine. No external implementation source has been established
for this mixer; the source's originality statement is retained, not treated
as an independently verified reconstruction of its creation history.

Chaos blur in shdsrc/vk_fog.frag is the project-labelled circular-aperture
gather: texture samples placed on a per-pixel rotated golden-angle spiral are
weighted by radial smoothstep and normalized by their weight sum, and its
region mask is feathered over the same spiral with Gaussian weights. With
Average in Gamma Space the samples are averaged after a 1/2.2 power and the
mean is raised back by 2.2; Average in Linear Space averages them as they are.
This generic display-gamma round trip is written here, not taken from a
source. The name does not identify a third-party library or a claim to
implement a particular published chaos algorithm.

The golden-angle spiral is related to the point arrangement published by H. Vogel,
"A better way to construct the sunflower head", Mathematical Biosciences 44
(1979) 179-189. The local radius is sqrt((i + 0.5)/n), and the angle is i
times the golden angle. The half-offset is the midpoint of each equal-area
annulus, as derived in the entry below; it is not attributed to Vogel's paper.
Similar short shader expressions alone do not establish a historical donor.

shdsrc/mfr_display.glsl and documentation/notes/mfr-hdr-preview.md identify the supplied
MFR-HDR 1.0 specification dated 2026-09-16 as the display equation source.
The tracked preview document records the shoulder/log/linear equations,
luminance coefficients, gamut compression and project-authored false-color
legend. The source labels this an independent implementation. The original
supplied specification's separate authorship/license has not been established
by this review; the preview is an implementation record, not a new license
grant for that specification. Existing color-standard references above apply
to the reused transforms. No reference HTML renderer is bundled by this entry.

